1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,304
Pick up Aziz's phone and look at it.

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:07,104
It is glass, a chip and a SIM card, and a million other phones look the same.

3
00:00:07,154 --> 00:00:10,272
So how does the platform know that this phone is Aziz's?

4
00:00:10,322 --> 00:00:15,384
In Lesson 19, the platform linked Aziz to the bank's own customer records.

5
00:00:15,434 --> 00:00:22,728
This lesson looks at the thing in his hand: the device record, its states, the check at each request, and the app PIN.

6
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:26,160
Go back to the day Aziz registered in the mobile bank.

7
00:00:26,210 --> 00:00:30,360
When his account was made, the platform also made a record for his phone.

8
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:33,120
It gave the phone a random identifier.

9
00:00:33,170 --> 00:00:37,008
The app keeps this identifier and sends it with every request.

10
00:00:37,058 --> 00:00:41,568
The identifier is never made from the serial number or any other hardware value.

11
00:00:41,618 --> 00:00:45,120
It is random, so it tells nobody anything about the phone.

12
00:00:45,170 --> 00:00:46,992
Now look inside the record.

13
00:00:47,042 --> 00:00:48,864
It keeps the customer reference.

14
00:00:48,914 --> 00:00:54,168
It keeps Aziz's personal identification number, in a form that cannot be reversed.

15
00:00:54,218 --> 00:00:59,232
It keeps the platform, the model, the version of the system and the version of the app.

16
00:00:59,282 --> 00:01:03,000
It keeps the time the record was made, and the time the phone was last seen.

17
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:04,872
And it keeps a state.

18
00:01:04,922 --> 00:01:08,736
One identifier belongs to one customer for its whole life.

19
00:01:08,786 --> 00:01:10,968
It never moves to a second person.

20
00:01:11,018 --> 00:01:14,328
On registration day, Aziz was at level one.

21
00:01:14,378 --> 00:01:16,992
So his phone got the state Known, not Trusted.

22
00:01:17,042 --> 00:01:21,144
Known means that the platform has seen this phone with this customer before.

23
00:01:21,194 --> 00:01:23,136
It says nothing about the person.

24
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:28,776
A week later, Aziz passed MyID on this same phone, and his status became Confirmed.

25
00:01:28,826 --> 00:01:32,640
At that moment the record changed: the phone became Trusted.

26
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:36,672
Only a MyID check that ends with Confirmed makes a device trusted.

27
00:01:36,722 --> 00:01:39,096
No code and no password can do it.

28
00:01:39,146 --> 00:01:40,800
Next, the app PIN.

29
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:41,040
After the first login on a phone, the app asks Aziz to set an app PIN.

30
00:01:41,090 --> 00:01:43,656
The app is not usable until he does.

31
00:01:43,706 --> 00:01:45,912
The length is a policy of the bank.

32
00:01:45,962 --> 00:01:51,624
The app refuses easy PINs, like a simple run of digits, or one digit again and again.

33
00:01:51,674 --> 00:01:53,712
Here is the part I like most.

34
00:01:53,762 --> 00:01:56,112
The PIN never leaves the phone.

35
00:01:56,162 --> 00:02:02,136
The platform records only that a PIN was set, when, and on which device.

36
00:02:02,186 --> 00:02:04,488
Only the phone checks the PIN.

37
00:02:04,538 --> 00:02:09,024
The platform never accepts a PIN as proof of anything.

38
00:02:09,074 --> 00:02:16,392
And the PIN is not the password: the password opens a login, the PIN only unlocks the app on this phone.

39
00:02:16,442 --> 00:02:18,264
I think this split is honest.

40
00:02:18,314 --> 00:02:24,432
A short PIN is good for the hand, and weak for a server, so the server never trusts it.

41
00:02:24,482 --> 00:02:26,304
Now the threat.

42
00:02:26,354 --> 00:02:29,712
A stranger gets hold of Aziz's phone for a few minutes.

43
00:02:29,762 --> 00:02:33,432
He copies the app's tokens and puts them in the app on his own phone.

44
00:02:33,482 --> 00:02:38,496
His app sends a request with the copied tokens, and with its own device identifier.

45
00:02:38,546 --> 00:02:40,560
This is where the binding check works.

46
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:44,928
At each request, the customer gateway asks three questions.

47
00:02:44,978 --> 00:02:47,520
First, is this device Trusted or Known?

48
00:02:47,570 --> 00:02:51,672
Second, does this device belong to the customer of this request?

49
00:02:51,722 --> 00:02:57,744
Third, is the personal identification number bound to this device the same as the customer's number?

50
00:02:57,794 --> 00:03:00,576
The stranger's phone fails the second question.

51
00:03:00,626 --> 00:03:02,448
It is not Aziz's device.

52
00:03:02,498 --> 00:03:04,320
One no is enough.

53
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:06,576
The access grant ends at once.

54
00:03:06,626 --> 00:03:10,632
The device becomes Revoked, with the reason binding mismatch.

55
00:03:10,682 --> 00:03:15,192
The platform writes a security event, and the app must start a new login.

56
00:03:15,242 --> 00:03:17,760
A revoked device stays revoked.

57
00:03:17,810 --> 00:03:19,632
It never becomes trusted again.

58
00:03:19,682 --> 00:03:25,512
If someone logs in again on that same phone, the platform makes a new record, which starts from the beginning.

59
00:03:25,562 --> 00:03:31,752
An operator of the bank can also revoke one device, or all the devices of a customer, with a reason.

60
00:03:31,802 --> 00:03:36,120
The grant of a revoked device ends at its next request, not later.

61
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:39,576
So the check runs on every request, not once a day.

62
00:03:39,626 --> 00:03:48,312
To recap: a random identifier, three states, one check at each request, and a PIN that never leaves the phone.

63
00:03:48,362 --> 00:03:50,184
Known means seen before.

64
00:03:50,234 --> 00:03:52,944
Trusted means MyID passed on this device.

65
00:03:52,994 --> 00:03:54,744
Revoked is final.

66
00:03:54,794 --> 00:03:57,432
But the binding check needs something to check.

67
00:03:57,482 --> 00:04:01,896
At each request, the app shows the gateway more than an identifier.

68
00:04:01,946 --> 00:04:04,536
What does the app actually hold to call the platform?

69
00:04:04,586 --> 00:04:06,672
Next: access grants.
